The fix has been successfully applied and verified by the build. Here's a summary of the changes:

## Bug Fix Summary

**File:** `app/src/main/java/io/lerk/lrkFM/activities/FileActivity.java`

**Root Cause:** Both `loadDirectory()` and `loadArchive()` methods create a **new** `ArrayAdapter` and call `fileListView.setAdapter()` every time the directory is reloaded (e.g., after file operations). Setting a new adapter resets the ListView's scroll position to the top (position 0, offset 0).

**Fix:** Save the scroll position before reloading and restore it after setting the new adapter.

### Patch:

```diff
--- a/app/src/main/java/io/lerk/lrkFM/activities/FileActivity.java
+++ b/app/src/main/java/io/lerk/lrkFM/activities/FileActivity.java
@@ -604,6 +604,12 @@
         FileLoader fileLoader = new FileLoader(path);
         View errorText = findViewById(R.id.unableToLoadText);
         View emptyText = findViewById(R.id.emptyDirText);
+
+        // Save scroll position before reloading so we can restore it after
+        int scrollPosition = fileListView.getFirstVisiblePosition();
+        View v = fileListView.getChildAt(0);
+        int scrollOffset = (v == null) ? 0 : v.getTop();
+
         try {
             files = fileLoader.loadLocationFiles();
             fileListView.setVisibility(VISIBLE);
@@ -612,6 +618,9 @@

             arrayAdapter = new FileArrayAdapter(this, R.layout.layout_file, sortFilesByPreference(files, new PrefUtils<String>(SORT_FILES_BY).getValue()));
             fileListView.setAdapter(arrayAdapter);
+
+            // Restore scroll position after setting the new adapter
+            fileListView.setSelectionFromTop(scrollPosition, scrollOffset);
         } catch (NoAccessException e) {
             Log.w(TAG, "Can't read '" + path + "': Permission denied!");
             fileListView.setVisibility(GONE);
@@ -650,6 +659,12 @@
         View errorText = findViewById(R.id.unableToLoadText);
         View emptyText = findViewById(R.id.emptyDirText);

+        // Save scroll position before reloading so we can restore it after
+        int scrollPosition = fileListView.getFirstVisiblePosition();
+        View v = fileListView.getChildAt(0);
+        int scrollOffset = (v == null) ? 0 : v.getTop();
+
         files = loader.loadLocationFiles();
         fileListView.setVisibility(VISIBLE);
         errorText.setVisibility(GONE);
@@ -657,6 +672,9 @@

         arrayAdapter = new ArchiveArrayAdapter(this, R.layout.layout_file, sortFilesByPreference(files, new PrefUtils<String>(SORT_FILES_BY).getValue()), archive);
         fileListView.setAdapter(arrayAdapter);
+
+        // Restore scroll position after setting the new adapter
+        fileListView.setSelectionFromTop(scrollPosition, scrollOffset);

         currentDirectory = path;
         historyMap.put(historyCounter++, new HistoryEntry(currentDirectory, archive));
```

**How it works:**
1. **Save:** Before creating the new adapter, capture `getFirstVisiblePosition()` (the index of the topmost visible item) and `getChildAt(0).getTop()` (the pixel offset of that item from the top of the ListView)
2. **Restore:** After `setAdapter()` is called, use `setSelectionFromTop(scrollPosition, scrollOffset)` to reposition the list to approximately the same location

This approach handles both normal scrolling (where the top item may be partially visible) and edge cases (first item at position 0 with no offset). The fix applies to both regular directories (`loadDirectory`) and archive browsing (`loadArchive`).

